@page "/Home"

<MudText Class="mx-4 px-4 mt-4" Typo="Typo.h2">OSDC Earth Gravity</MudText>
<MudDivider Class="mb-4" />
<MudText Class="mx-6 px-6" Typo="Typo.body1">
    This stateless microservice evaluates EGM96 total gravity at WGS84 positions. Public API values always use SI:
    latitude and longitude are radians, and depth is metres positive downward from the WGS84 reference ellipsoid.
    The web interface can display and accept other units through the OSDC unit-reference system while preserving SI
    on the API boundary. Depth is a position, not an interval length. Gravity components are signed
    north, east and down accelerations; their magnitude is nonnegative. Total potential includes both
    gravitational and centrifugal contributions and is expressed in m�/s�.
</MudText>

<MudGrid Class="mx-4 px-4 mt-2">
    <MudItem xs="12" md="6">
        <MudPaper Class="pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Why gravity varies by location</MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
                Two effects change the pull of gravity as you move across the globe. First, the Earth is not a
                sphere: it bulges at the equator and flattens at the poles, and it spins, so a point at the equator
                is both farther from the centre of mass and subject to more centrifugal acceleration than a point at
                the poles. Together these make gravity roughly 0.5% weaker at the equator than at the poles.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
                Second, the Earth's mass is not evenly distributed. Mountain ranges, ocean trenches, sedimentary
                basins, and denser or lighter rock in the crust and mantle all add small local pulls or deficits on
                top of the smooth ellipsoidal trend. These are <b>gravity anomalies</b>, and mapping them is one of
                the main uses of a global gravity model.
            </MudText>
        </MudPaper>
    </MudItem>
    <MudItem xs="12" md="6">
        <MudPaper Class="pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Why gravity varies with depth</MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
                Gravity also changes with elevation. Moving away from the WGS84 ellipsoid — upward, in the
                free-air sense — weakens gravity because you are farther from the bulk of the Earth's mass; moving
                toward it, including to modest depths below the surface such as a wellbore, strengthens it slightly
                for the same reason, before deeper effects such as the mass enclosed above you start to matter.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
                EGM96 is a model of the gravity field <i>outside</i> the Earth's masses. This service evaluates it by
                converting the requested SI <code>Depth</code> to an ellipsoidal height (<code>height = −Depth</code>)
                and downward-continuing the same spherical-harmonic solution, the standard practice for near-surface
                engineering use such as borehole surveys; it does not model the Earth's internal density structure.
            </MudText>
        </MudPaper>
    </MudItem>
    <MudItem xs="12" md="6">
        <MudPaper Class="pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">How gravity is estimated: EGM96</MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
                <b>EGM96</b> (Earth Gravitational Model 1996) represents the Earth's gravitational potential as a
                spherical-harmonic expansion up to degree and order 360, fit jointly to satellite tracking data,
                surface and shipborne gravimetry, and satellite altimetry over the oceans. Combined with the
                ellipsoid's own centrifugal term, evaluating the expansion and its gradient at a position gives the
                total gravity vector and potential there.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
                This service loads the model once at startup using <b>GeographicLib</b>'s implementation. Exact
                model identity, degree/order, publisher, release date, and a SHA-256 of the coefficient file are
                available on the <MudLink Href="/EarthGravity/webapp/EarthGravityModel">Gravity model</MudLink> page
                and via the model-information API and MCP tool.
            </MudText>
        </MudPaper>
    </MudItem>
    <MudItem xs="12" md="6">
        <MudPaper Class="pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
            <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Why this matters</MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
                Systems that sense or rely on the direction and strength of gravity need the local value, not a
                single global average. Inertial navigation and gyroscopic surveying instruments — including
                measurement-while-drilling and gyro tools used to orient a wellbore — resolve their attitude against
                the local gravity vector, so an error in the assumed gravity translates directly into a position or
                orientation error.
            </MudText>
            <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
                Geophysical surveys likewise use the local anomaly, after removing the smooth ellipsoidal trend, to
                infer subsurface density contrasts. This microservice exists so that any consuming application can
                get a consistent, provenance-tracked gravity vector for a WGS84 position without embedding its own
                gravity model.
            </MudText>
        </MudPaper>
    </MudItem>
</MudGrid>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">OSDC convention</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        Latitude and longitude are always WGS84 geodetic coordinates in SI radians. <code>Depth</code> is SI metres,
        positive downward from the WGS84 reference ellipsoid; a negative depth is above the ellipsoid. Depth is
        never referenced to mean sea level, a geoid, seabed, rig datum, or local vertical datum — use the
        <MudLink Href="https://github.com/Open-Source-Drilling-Community/EarthVerticalDatum">Earth Vertical Datum</MudLink>
        microservice first if a conversion from one of those references is needed.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        The returned gravity vector is expressed in the local <b>north-east-down (NED)</b> frame, in SI m/s²: North
        is positive northward, East is positive eastward, and Down is positive toward the Earth and therefore
        normally positive for gravity. <code>Magnitude</code> is the non-negative norm of the vector, and
        <code>TotalPotential</code> — in SI m²/s² — is the combined gravitational and centrifugal potential at that
        position. Total gravity always includes the centrifugal acceleration of Earth's rotation.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
        Every calculation request is stateless and atomic: input positions and results are not persisted, all
        positions in a batch are validated together, and one invalid position rejects the complete request rather
        than returning a partial result. Only cumulative operational counters are retained by the service. The web
        interface can display and accept other units through the OSDC unit-reference system, but SI radians and SI
        metres are always what cross the API boundary.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Using this web application</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-2">
        Open <MudLink Href="/EarthGravity/webapp/EarthGravityCalculation">Evaluate gravity</MudLink>, enter a WGS84
        latitude and longitude — as sexagesimal or decimal degrees — and a depth below the WGS84 ellipsoid, then
        press <i>Evaluate</i>. The input and result fields follow the unit system selected for the page, while the
        service itself always exchanges SI radians and SI metres. The result panel reports the north, east, and down
        gravity components, the vector magnitude, the total potential, and the identity of the model used.
    </MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1">
        The <MudLink Href="/EarthGravity/webapp/EarthGravityModel">Gravity model</MudLink> page shows the loaded
        model's identity, degree/order, publisher, release date, and coefficient checksum. The
        <MudLink Href="/EarthGravity/webapp/StatisticsEarthGravity">Usage statistics</MudLink> page shows cumulative
        request counters retained across pod restarts when the service's persistent <code>/home</code> volume is retained.
    </MudText>
</MudPaper>

<MudPaper Class="mx-4 px-4 pa-6 mt-4 mb-6" Elevation="2">
    <MudText Typo="Typo.h5" Class="mb-3">Access methods</MudText>
    <MudText Typo="Typo.body1" Class="mb-3">
        The same evaluation is reachable through several interfaces built on the same stateless model, so it can be
        used interactively or wired into other systems and AI agents.
    </MudText>
    <MudSimpleTable>
        <thead>
            <tr><th>Method</th><th>Description</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
            <tr>
                <td><b>Web page</b></td>
                <td>
                    This site — a server-side Blazor application (this page) for manual, one-off evaluations and for
                    inspecting the loaded gravity model and usage statistics.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><b>REST API</b></td>
                <td>
                    JSON endpoints under <code>/EarthGravity/api/EarthGravity</code> for discovery, model
                    information, and synchronous batch evaluation, documented and explorable through the Swagger UI
                    at <code>/EarthGravity/api/swagger</code>. Suited to scripts, backend integration, and any HTTP
                    client.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><b>MCP</b></td>
                <td>
                    A Model Context Protocol server exposed as Streamable HTTP at
                    <code>/EarthGravity/api/mcp</code>, publishing three tools — connectivity <code>ping</code>,
                    <code>earth_gravity_get_model_info</code>, and <code>earth_gravity_evaluate</code> — with strict
                    input/output JSON Schemas, so AI agents and MCP-aware tooling can call the same evaluations
                    directly.
                </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
                <td><b>Reusable web pages</b></td>
                <td>
                    The Razor components behind the "Evaluate gravity" and "Gravity model" pages are published as the
                    <code>OSDC.Drilling.EarthGravity.WebPages</code> NuGet package, so other Blazor applications can
                    embed the same unit-aware controls instead of re-implementing this UI.
                </td>
            </tr>
        </tbody>
    </MudSimpleTable>
</MudPaper>
